Give the recorder a whole physical core, not half of one.

**Patch 0008 pinned a hyperthread.** It put the recorder on `cpu0` and every
child of this script on `1-7`, on the understanding that this box has eight
cores. It has four:

    model name : Intel(R) Core(TM) i7-4930MX CPU @ 3.00GHz
    /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu0/topology/thread_siblings_list -> 0-1

`cpu1` is `cpu0`'s sibling. An MPI rank there shares the recorder's execution
units, its L1 and its L2 -- so the recorder had a dedicated *thread* on a core
somebody else was using, which is not what 0008 claims and not what it needs.

**What it cost, measured over 10.3 hours** by `chirp-drop-watch` (2026-08-12
23:00 to 2026-08-13 09:15 UTC, 41 windows of 900 s):

    eleven consecutive windows   drops 0        RcvbufErrors +0
    10.3 h mean                  drops 14.4/s   RcvbufErrors +2280/s
    worst window                 drops 73/s     RcvbufErrors +9811/s

Zero from 00:30 to 02:45 UTC with the counter frozen, then onset at 03:45 and a
monotone climb all morning -- tracking when the consumers have work, which on
this station means when the band opens. **0008 is real** (the unpinned station
never produced a hard zero) **and incomplete**.

**Why it is throughput, not latency.** Patch 0005 got UHD its full 500 MB
socket buffer, confirmed:

    net.core.rmem_max = 500000000
    Creating the USRP device with: recv_buff_size=500000000     (no resize warning)

500 MB is **five seconds** of stream at 100 MB/s, so `RcvbufErrors` cannot move
unless that buffer is full. 2,280/s is 3.36 MB/s of 100 MB/s -- **3.4%**. A
scheduling stall drops everything for its duration and nothing either side; a
steady 3.4% with the buffer pinned full is a recorder running at 96.6% of line
rate. Half a physical core produces exactly that. This is why 0008 improved
drops by 96% but `RcvbufErrors` by only 81%.

**Four candidates eliminated by direct measurement first**, all during a lossy
window, because three of them are what anyone would reach for:

| candidate | reading | verdict |
|---|---|---|
| load | zero windows 5.01-6.60, lossy 4.90-8.52; corr +0.37 | ranges overlap -- not load |
| memory reclaim | `allocstall +0`, `pgscan_direct +0`, `pswpin +7` pages / 60 s | out; the 7.3 GB in swap is history |
| thermal | `package_throttle_count` **+0 in 60 s** (451,688 lifetime) | out, and it was a serious candidate |
| socket buffer | 500 MB confirmed above | out |

**What changes.** The shell mask goes `1-7` -> `2-7`, and the recorder goes
`-c 0` -> `-c 0-1`. Both siblings for the recorder rather than `cpu0` alone:
they share L1 and L2, so a thread moving between them costs nothing, and
leaving `cpu1` empty would only deny the recorder's own helper threads a place
to run. The consumers lose one hyperthread, worth roughly 0.3 of a core.

**The caveat that has to travel with this.** One diurnal cycle cannot fully
separate "the consumers got busy" from "the sun came up". Thermal -- the main
rival -- was eliminated by measurement, and the topology is not in question.
But the mechanism rests on a correlation across a single day. **The log after
this patch is what settles it**: if the sibling was the cause, tomorrow's
morning windows go to zero at the same detection volumes that cost 20,000 drops
today. Check before believing:

    sudo tail -40 /var/lib/chirp-drop-watch/drop-watch.log

Apply on top of 0008:

    cd ~/chirpsounder2
    git apply --check /path/to/0010-dombas-give-the-recorder-a-physical-core.patch
    git apply         /path/to/0010-dombas-give-the-recorder-a-physical-core.patch

Takes effect on the next full `dombas.sh` launch; a running recorder keeps the
mask it started with. **Do not run either changed line by hand.** The second is
the recorder's launch -- in an interactive shell it starts a *second* recorder
against a USRP the live one owns, and its `>` truncates `thor.log`.

To verify after the next launch, without touching anything:

    taskset -pc $(pgrep -f rx_uhd_ext_gps | head -1)      # want 0-1
    taskset -pc $(pgrep -f detect_chirps.py | head -1)    # want 2-7

